  <ym-title|Independent review: finite-menu lattice
  comparison><label|independent-review-finite-menu-lattice-comparison>

  Reviewed <verbatim|chapters/14-lattice-comparison.tex> against the current
  Chapter 11 proposition <verbatim|ch11:n-mark-comparison> and its common
  extraction/window covariance suppliers.

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The polynomial map T and its same-insertion covariance are defined
    at the common reading level. They do not depend on the later
    magnification exponent. One longest restarted history can be restricted
    for the finite menu; no different top law or spectator-dependent
    coefficient extraction is introduced.

    <item>The full n-marker algebra retains all incoming joint modes.
    Dividing by the finite free moment polynomial is an exact coefficient
    identity with unchanged empty coefficient. Bounds depend on fixed marks
    per cell and their finite subsets, not the number of reflected cells. The
    general n Wick/log estimates weaken the covariance exponent
    appropriately.

    <item>Restored source amplitude H^(1/(8n)) and normalization H^(1/(16n))
    give the announced negative root exponent for every nonempty mode. The
    leading and lower scale ratios, with the finite stage delay, give
    physical cell side at most a_k H^(b+5).

    <item>The lift relation at different magnifications is exact: the
    engineering dilation composes, and it commutes with factor scaling. The
    inverse bound is needed only uniformly in N,p at each fixed reading
    level; no uniform bound in k is falsely claimed.

    <item>The four exponent inequalities admit the stated choice. Enlarging
    the common part of all three alpha values preserves the first three
    inequalities and makes the last negative. They also imply s_k/delta_k
    tends to zero, including the degree-zero case. The normalization r_k
    s_k=a_k is correct.

    <item>The first-scale cells fit within fixed separated configurations;
    the second-scale cells fit within the strip margin r_k delta_k. The
    positional derivative is with respect to rescaled coordinates, giving
    1/delta_k rather than 1/(r_k delta_k). Coordinate-hybrid strips still
    have volume O(delta_k). Uniform covariance estimates depend on full point
    separation, not on a nonzero difference in every coordinate.

    <item>Taking bounded coefficient limit points at each fixed k does not
    change the previously chosen full cutoff correlation limit. The
    finite-menu diagonal sequence is compatible with the original field
    family and its real/unit/signed identities.
  </itemize-minus>

  No concrete correction found in this targeted review. It relies on the
  actual restarted-family proof, whose recording versus actual coupling issue
  was independently corrected and audited. It does not replace the earlier
  original-source verification of the finite Gaussian transport and
  same-history local compiler.
